title: Building a release
tags: Development
kind: guide
audience: dev
aliases: release make-release
summary: make release writes dist/metafolder-<version>-<target>.tar.gz — every program, the shipped defaults laid out as the configuration and an install.sh — for a machine without a checkout.

A release is an archive that installs metafolder where there is no source
checkout. It is built from a checkout:

```
make release            # scripts/dev/release.sh; DIST=<dir> writes elsewhere than dist/
```

The script runs `make build build-watchd docs` — every program in release, the
frontend bundled first, and the rendered documentation — then lays out
`dist/metafolder-<version>-<target>/` and archives it. The version is the one in
`crates/core/Cargo.toml`, the target the host `rustc` names.

|!In the archive |!What |
|`bin/` |`metafolder-daemon`, `mf`, `metafolder-gui`, `metafolder-sync-config`, `metafolder-watchd` |
|`share/metafolder/defaults/` |the shipped defaults, laid out as the configuration is, written by `metafolder-sync-config --source <checkout> --export <dir>` |
|`share/metafolder/watchd/` |the broker's systemd unit and system user (`scripts/dev/metafolder-watchd.*`) |
|`install.sh`, `README` |from `scripts/dev/release/` |

!! Installing one

```
tar xzf metafolder-0.3.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz
cd metafolder-0.3.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
./install.sh                 # into ~/.local; --prefix DIR elsewhere
./install.sh --watchd        # also the fanotify broker, a system service (sudo)
```

`install.sh` copies the programs to `<prefix>/bin`, replaces
`<prefix>/share/metafolder/defaults/` whole (a default the new release no longer
ships does not stay), then runs `metafolder-sync-config` with no argument, which
applies the defaults beside it to `~/.config/metafolder/`: the first time it
creates the configuration, after an upgrade it merges the new defaults into your
edits ([[Updating the configuration]]). The broker never goes under the prefix:
`--watchd` installs it root-owned in `/usr/local/bin`, as `make install-watchd`
does ([[The fanotify broker]]).

!! Why a directory beside the programs

The defaults are files next to the binaries, not embedded in
`metafolder-sync-config`: that is where a package puts a program's data
(`/usr/share/<name>/`), they stay readable, and `metafolder-sync-config` already
read a directory. `--export` writes them in the layout of the configuration
itself, so the mapping from the checkout's `crates/*/default-config/` is written
once, in `gather_defaults` ([[Shipped defaults in the source tree]]); the export
applied gives the same `default` tree as the checkout it came from
(`config_sync::tests`). With no `--source` or `--defaults`,
`metafolder-sync-config` reads `<dir of the binary>/../share/metafolder/defaults/`
and nothing else: a package installing `/usr/bin` and `/usr/share/metafolder/`
needs no argument either.

The archive is not a distribution package: it carries no list of the libraries
the GUI links (webkit2gtk, gtk3) nor of the tools it calls
([[GUI runtime dependencies]]); the `README` names them. `scripts/dev/test-release.sh` holds the
layout of the archive and what `install.sh` does, against stubs.
